{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b17", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Semiconductors - Test 1", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d6db"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d696", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d62f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b4798"], "topics": ["6117b5e6478ae845c43c5ed6"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5de5"], "question": {"text": "Consider a situation in which reverse biased current of a particular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> junction increases when it is exposed to a light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8804;</mo><mn>621</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;During this process, enhancement in carrier concentration takes place due to generation of hole-electron pairs. The value of band gap is nearly."}, "options": [{"id": "161384", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d630"}, {"id": "aec04d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d631"}, {"id": "776022", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d632"}, {"id": "81debf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d633"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d634", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b4798"], "topics": ["6117b650478ae845c43c6900"], "concepts": ["6162fb656ed13040ec84e61a"], "question": {"text": "Consider a n-type semiconductor in which $\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{e}}$ and $\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{h}}$ are number of electrons and holes, respectively.<br/>(A) Holes are minority carriers<br/>(B) The dopant is a pentavalent atom<br/>(C) $\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{e}} \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{h}} \\neq \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{i}}^2$<br/>(where $n_i$ is number of electrons or holes in semiconductor when it is intrinsic form)<br/>(D) $\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{e}} \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{h}} \\geq \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{i}}^2$<br/>(E) The holes are not generated due to the donors Choose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "3528cc", "text": "(A), (C), (D) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d635"}, {"id": "4a0fae", "text": "(A), (C), (E) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d636"}, {"id": "ae4c03", "text": "(A), (B), (E) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d637"}, {"id": "397f0e", "text": "(A), (B), (C) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d638"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d639", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b4798"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4759"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e39"], "question": {"text": "The circuit shown below contains two ideal diodes, each with a forward resistance of $50 \\Omega$. If the battery voltage is $6 \\mathrm{~V},$ the current through the $100 \\Omega$ resistance (in Amperes) is:\n\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F81YotldGrsYQ52emafYDzohLgdJbl0BxJYWxrE-HJlU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">"}, "options": [{"id": "b7d721", "text": "0.036", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d63a"}, {"id": "5a4a1b", "text": "0.02", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d63b"}, {"id": "82c151", "text": "0.027", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d63c"}, {"id": "b13c60", "text": "0.03", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d63d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d63e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b4798"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5670"], "concepts": ["667d30e214e932e9dc100312"], "question": {"text": "The $I-V$ characteristics of an electronic device shown in the figure. The device is:\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FOR3DNtVaNFBGdwDvZ4nuYU-HoZYTFCLoAg6SnCUaELs.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>"}, "options": [{"id": "5585d1", "text": "a diode which can be used as a rectifier", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d63f"}, {"id": "ab13aa", "text": "a zener diode which can be used as a voltage regulator", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d640"}, {"id": "832778", "text": "a transistor which can be used as an amplifier", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d641"}, {"id": "8b9088", "text": "a solar cell", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d642"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d643", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b4798"], "topics": ["60e567c5499d1e49588f9963"], "concepts": ["60e56813499d1e49588fa392"], "question": {"text": "<p>Name the logic gate equivalent to the diagram attached</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F06cb392d-6968-474c-9877-9fbf1ce81c02-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 249px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "c7802f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NAND</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d644"}, {"id": "bdcc74", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AND</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d645"}, {"id": "5836e1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NOR</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d646"}, {"id": "cff28b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>OR</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d647"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d648", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b4798"], "topics": ["60e5673b499d1e49588f8b82"], "concepts": ["6162fb676ed13040ec84e7e1"], "question": {"text": "Statement I : To get a steady DC output from the pulsating voltage received from a full wave rectifier we can connect a capacitor across the output parallel to the load <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></math><br />Statement II : To get a steady DC output from the pulsating voltage received from a full wave rectifier we can connect an inductor in series with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></math><br />In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "eabdab", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d649"}, {"id": "253456", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d64a"}, {"id": "83b647", "text": "Statement I is false but Statement II is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d64b"}, {"id": "2cc708", "text": "Statement I&nbsp;is true but Statement II is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d64c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d64d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b4798"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4759"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b1d"], "question": {"text": "<p>Take the breakdown voltage of the zener diode used in the given circuit as <math><mrow><mn>6</mn><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></math>. For the input voltage shown in the figure below, the time variation of the output voltage is: (Graphs drawn are schematic and not to the scale)</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Ff4bb1330-f2df-47da-9ed3-5385aa53e6fd-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 667px; height: 198px;\" />&nbsp;</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "6cc4ce", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F920f44a9-0b33-4f94-8774-17a2b8eb0631-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 465px; height: 191px;\" />&nbsp;", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d64e"}, {"id": "c932ac", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F023742f2-fb4e-4f9c-9c56-9a43988fabe0-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 465px; height: 191px;\" />&nbsp;&nbsp;", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d64f"}, {"id": "50b8c9", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fa751f833-be2a-47e5-9134-4aee58e2d995-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 465px; height: 187px;\" />", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d650"}, {"id": "6de37c", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F0f84010e-7e79-4d02-bf50-1daeb9e89bbf-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 465px; height: 187px;\" />", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d651"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d652", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b4798"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5670"], "concepts": ["6117b88f67dbe2603c3e80a9"], "question": {"text": "The photodiode is used to detect the optical signals. These diodes are preferably operated in reverse biased mode because"}, "options": [{"id": "5d12a5", "text": "fractional change in majority carriers produce higher forward bias current", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d653"}, {"id": "f61f0c", "text": "fractional change in majority carriers produce higher reverse bias current", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d654"}, {"id": "5838cb", "text": "fractional change in minority carriers produce higher forward bias current", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d655"}, {"id": "8c932f", "text": "fractional change in minority carriers produce higher reverse bias current", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d656"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f1e2ee0e063d923d657", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60e2fadb66f9ce3f243b4798"], "topics": ["6117b5e6478ae845c43c5ed6"], "concepts": ["63f482b98f91a02cddf9f219"], "question": {"text": "<p>Match the List I with List II</p><table border